Probability-Based Word Predictor Using N-Gram Models (Uni and bigram model)

Introduction 

Language prediction is the task of predicting what word is likely to occur next in a sequence of words. It is used in applications such as autocomplete, search 
suggestions, text editors, speech recognition, and conversational systems. The objective of this project is to build a simple probability-based word predictor in
Python. Instead of using a neural network, the initial version uses fundamental concepts from probability and statistics to understand how word prediction can be 
performed. The project begins with a unigram probability model and is then extended to a bigram model, where the previous word is used as context for predicting 
the next word.

Given a sequence of words, the objective is to predict the most probable next word.

For example:
I want to learn ___

A word predictor should estimate the probability of possible next words and select the word with the highest probability.
Mathematically:

w^*= argmax P(w|mid context) 

For a bigram model, the context consists of one previous word:

w^∗ = argwmaxP(w∣wi−1)

